From: quickjs-ng bundle work
Subject: Register VM: split argv_safe from args_captured (P6)

What this does
  Fixes a memory-safety regression from 0182/0185. Those computed b->argv_safe
  from regvm_scan_argv_safe, which only checked the args_captured conditions (a
  var_ref onto a parameter, or the mapped arguments object). But argv_safe has a
  second consumer: JS_CallInternal uses it
  ((flags & JS_CALL_FLAG_COPY_ARGV) && !JS_ARGV_SAFE(b)) to decide whether a C
  caller's argv (JS_Call / JS_CallFree / JS_CallConstructor*) may be used in
  place. A function that writes a parameter was therefore reported argv-safe,
  its put_arg stored straight into the caller's borrowed array, and the caller
  later freed a value it no longer owned (free_zero_refcount assertion).

  regvm_scan_argv_safe is replaced by regvm_scan_arg_flags(code, len,
  &argv_safe, &args_captured), which computes the two flags separately, as the
  stack scan does:
    args_captured  a var_ref onto a parameter slot (ROP_make_local_ref kind 1)
                   or the mapped arguments object -- unchanged;
    argv_safe      false for args_captured, or for any of ROP_put_arg,
                   ROP_put_arg_xfer, ROP_eval, ROP_apply_eval, ROP_get_arg_el,
                   ROP_apply_arguments -- the register counterparts of every
                   opcode js_argv_safe_opcode rejects.
  The fused decoder (P3) computes both in its single pass, and the three scan
  sites (js_create_function, the eager read, materialization) use the new
  function. The stack scan is refactored into js_scan_arg_flags_raw so the
  debug cross-check can run on fd->byte_code even after P5 stops installing it.

Why we need it
  argv_safe is read in every build, not only by the stack VM as the P2 note
  claimed. Reporting a parameter-writing function safe lets it clobber a C
  caller's argv and corrupt reference counts.

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      regvm_arg_ref_op / regvm_op_writes_args / regvm_argv_safe_op /
      regvm_scan_arg_flags; js_scan_arg_flags_raw + the js_scan_argv_safe
      refactor; the decoder's two-flag computation; the js_create_function,
      eager-read and materialization updates; the debug cross-check.

  Repro/gate: .local/regvm-bundle/argv-clobber.c calls ten parameter-writing
  shapes through JS_Call and checks argv[0]. Before: cases 1, 2 and 4 print
  CLOBBERED and teardown aborts. After: all ten ok with clean ASan teardown, in
  source, eager-bundle and lazy-bundle modes (the script is round-tripped
  through JS_WriteObject/JS_ReadObject for the latter two).

  Debug cross-check: the register scan may be MORE permissive than the stack
  scan, because the lowering elides dead argument stores (a parameter the stack
  code writes back to arg_buf but the register code keeps in a register, e.g.
  `function(n){ var s=0; while(n>0){ s+=n; n--; } return s; }`). The check
  therefore asserts the sound direction, `stack argv_safe => register
  argv_safe`, plus exact args_captured equality; the P6 bug itself (a register
  scan that misses a put_arg) is caught by the argv-clobber gate. Run over the
  differential suite (400/0/0) and test262 in a debug build.

  Gates: box2d 546.1008391043414 (source/eager/lazy); test262 50/81192 no
  leaks; differential corpus 400/0/0; 13 Octane rows clean.

